{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And they came to the threshing-floor of Atad.</b> It was surrounded by thorns. Now if it is the way of every threshing-floor to be surrounded by thorns, why was this one called 'the threshing-floor of Atad' more than all other threshing-floors? Was there then no threshing-floor in the world surrounded by thorns besides this one? And further, it is difficult what is stated (Genesis 50:11): 'And when the Canaanite, the inhabitant of the land, saw the mourning at the threshing-floor of Atad, they said: This is a grievous mourning to the Egyptians' — what do I need 'at the threshing-floor of Atad' for? For its implication is as though the threshing-floor of Atad caused the mourning. And further, why did the Egyptians mourn? Were they his sons or his relatives, that they mourned over him so with a whole heart? Or if they were such righteous people that their hearts burned within them and the passing of the righteous was distressing to them? And further, the expression 'grievous' does not fit the mourning." "And from here a proof appears, for what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Tosefta Sotah 13) that in the merit of Jacob the famine ceased, and when Jacob died the famine returned to its place. And therefore the Egyptians made a grievous mourning on account of the heaviness of the famine, which had returned to what it was at first, for the Egyptians sensed this thing — that the famine would return to its former state — by means of this sign that happened to them on the road: for suddenly there appeared before them one threshing-floor that was surrounded by thorns from every side, so that no opening or path remained to enter the threshing-floor. And from this they understood that the merit of the righteous had caused them, during his lifetime, that they had a path to go to the threshing-floor and to eat from its granary and its winepress; and at his death, the wicked, who are likened to thorns, would block them, so that they would no longer have a path to the threshing-floor as they had before, for their protective shade had departed from over them. And in the portion of Exodus, at the verse 'And they were repulsed because of the children of Israel' (Exodus 1:12), it will be explained with the help of YHWH that the Egyptians were likened to thorns in relation to the vineyard of YHWH of Hosts, the house of Israel. Therefore it was hinted to them that on account of the wickedness of the Egyptians the famine would return to its former state." "And in the manner that our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Bava Kamma 60a): punishment does not come to the world except on account of the wicked, and it does not begin except with the righteous, as it is stated (Exodus 22:5): 'When a fire goes forth and finds thorns' — these are the wicked — 'and consumes the stack' — that was already consumed. You see that the thorns and the stack mentioned there are the threshing-floor of Atad, for the threshing-floor is the stack and the thorns are the Atad. And He called the righteous one 'stack' because everyone eats in his merit, as they said (Ta'anit 10b): 'The whole world is sustained for the sake of Chanina my son,' etc., and as it is written (Leviticus 21:1–2): 'He shall not defile himself for the dead except for his close flesh' — that is, for the righteous one, for everyone is as his relatives and is called 'his flesh' on account of the closeness and on account of the fact that he sustains him, for 'flesh' means food. This is what is said: 'And it does not begin except with the righteous' — that is to say, this is the beginning of the punishment, that the Holy One, blessed be He, takes from the sinning generation the righteous one who used to feed and sustain the generation in his merit; and the punishment itself comes on account of the existence of the wicked, who are likened to thorns, and they cause that the stack was already consumed, for they no longer have a mouth to eat it, and the thorns block the path to the stack." "And when the Egyptians saw that this sign was performed for them — that suddenly YHWH brought before them one threshing-floor surrounded by thorns from every side, contrary to the way of the world — immediately they sensed that YHWH had fenced it off for them, so as not to give them access to the threshing-floor any more. Therefore the Egyptians mourned 'a grievous mourning,' meaning on account of the heaviness of the famine. And with this it is explained why they mourned specifically at the threshing-floor of Atad and not earlier. And when the Canaanite saw this mourning at the threshing-floor of Atad, and it was difficult for him why they had not made this mourning earlier, before they came to this place — it was surely because this is a grievous mourning for the Egyptians, for the heaviness of the famine would return to its place. Therefore it is specifically for the Egyptians and not for the sons of Jacob — and this is a correct and precious allusion." "And with this it appears to me possible to settle what is stated: 'And Joseph's brothers saw that their father was dead' (Genesis 50:15) — what did they see? And further, it is difficult: why did they not ask their father to command him in truth? And further, it is difficult what Joseph said: 'I will sustain you' (Genesis 50:21) — who asked this of his hand? Did they not ask anything but that he not do evil to them? Rather, surely Joseph was not suspected in their eyes of doing actual evil to them; but when they saw that Jacob had died and at his death the famine had returned to its former state, they were afraid that even if Joseph would not do evil to them, in any case there is reason to fear that perhaps he would not do them good either, and would not sustain them in the years of famine, and would repay us the evil that we did to him — not by rising and acting, but by sitting and not acting. Therefore Joseph needed to say to them: 'And now, do not fear — I will sustain you and your little ones' (Genesis 50:21) — you, for the sake of your little ones, for why should the little ones come to punishment, seeing that they did not sin at all?"], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 50, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:34:54.144603604Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}